  "query_preview": "..The upper low that brought severe weather to Arizona \nthe last two days has moved into the western Great Basin. Dewpoints \nremain elevated across the eastern half of the forecast area in the \nmid to upper 50s, with dewpoints in the mid to upper 40s across the \nwestern half of the state. Cloud cover continues to hang in from \nTucson eastward with clear skies across the western half of the \nstate. KEMX radar shows a few light returns/showers over eastern \nportions of the forecast area. Satellite layer blended PW values are \ncurrently around one-half inch west of Tucson and between 0.75-1.00 \ninches to the east of the Tucson.  \n\nThrough Saturday the main upper low will shift northeast into the \nnorthern tier of states, while a secondary lobe of energy will drop \ndown the back side of the trough into the desert southwest. This \ndeveloping upper low will move into far southeast California tonight \nand into western Arizona early Saturday. This system will then track \nnortheast across Arizona into southern Utah late Saturday night. \nThis system will not be like its predecessor, in that it will have \nneither strong jet dynamic support or a subtropical moisture plume \nto work with. However, there will still be enough moisture in place \nto work with the trough/upper low to bring a slight chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms over far eastern portions of the forecast \narea through Saturday. \n\nShortwave ridging and southwest flow aloft will then prevail Monday \nthrough Wednesday of next week, as a series of upper lows move into \nthe Pacific Northwest. This will result in dry and warmer conditions \nareawide across southeast Arizona. \n\nDuring the latter half of next week the deterministic GFS/ECMWF \nsolutions diverge significantly. The Ensembles favor west coast \ntroughing, which is reflected in our grids/forecast, with high \ntemperatures lowering below normal and a slight chance of showers \nand thunderstorms Thursday and a chance of showers/thunderstorms on \nFriday.   \n\nOverall, high temperatures will be 1-5 degrees below normal through \nMonday, near normal next Tuesday and Wednesday, lowering to 4-8 \ndegrees below normal by the end of next week. Near freezing to sub-\nfreezing morning lows look likely in the White Mountains and \nHannagan Meadow Sunday morning through next Tuesday morning.",
